@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Jumping") {
        In this part, we'll add the ability to jump. With the character able
        to leave the ground, we'll be ready to squash monsters by landing on
        them — which we'll implement in the next two lessons using hit boxes
        and hurt boxes.

        @Image(source: fix-me.png)
    }
    @Section(title: "Jumping") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The jumping mechanic itself requires only two lines of code. Open
            the _Player_ script. We need a value to control the jump's
            strength and update `_physics_process()` to code the jump.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                After the line that defines `fall_acceleration`, at the top
                of the script, add the `jump_impulse` property.

                Then, inside `_physics_process()`, add a check that
                applies the jump impulse when the player is on the
                floor and just pressed the "jump" action.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-07.gd, previousFile: player-06.gd)
            }
            That's all you need to jump!

            The `is_on_floor()` method is a tool from the `CharacterBody3D`
            class. It returns `true` if the body collided with the floor in
            this frame. That's why we apply gravity to the _Player_: so we
            collide with the floor instead of floating over it like the
            monsters.

            If the character is on the floor and the player presses "jump",
            we instantly give them a lot of vertical speed. In games, you
            really want controls to be responsive and giving instant speed
            boosts like these, while unrealistic, feels great.

            > Tip: Notice that the Y axis is positive upwards in 3D. That's
            > unlike 2D, where the Y axis is positive downwards. The
            > `jump_impulse` value is a positive Y velocity, so the
            > character moves up — not down.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Making the jump feel forgiving") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The jump works, but it's strict: press a frame too late after
            walking off a ledge and nothing happens. Press a frame too
            early while falling toward the ground and the press is
            swallowed. On a touch screen in particular, where input
            latency eats a few frames, that feels unfair.

            Two cheap tricks fix this. **Coyote time** keeps treating the
            player as "on the floor" for a brief moment after they leave
            it, so a slightly-late jump still works. **Jump buffering**
            remembers a too-early jump press for a brief moment and
            fires it the instant the player lands.

            Both are just a couple of `@export` properties and a couple
            of countdown timers. Read the comments in the code — they
            walk through each piece.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open the `player.gd` script. We add two `@export`
                properties — `coyote_time` and `jump_buffer` — so you
                can tune both windows in the _Inspector_ without
                touching code. We add two private timer variables to
                track them at runtime. Then we replace the strict
                `is_on_floor() and Input.is_action_just_pressed("jump")`
                check with a version that uses both timers.

                The jump now fires whenever there is both a *buffered*
                press and a *valid coyote window*. Clearing both timers
                on a successful jump prevents double-jumping off the
                coyote window.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-08.gd, previousFile: player-07.gd)
            }
            Try the game. Walk off the edge of the ground and press jump
            just after leaving — you'll still jump. Jump a hair too
            early while falling onto a snake's head and you'll still
            bounce. The game feels forgiving without becoming
            overpowered.

            > Tip: Both `coyote_time` and `jump_buffer` default to
            > `0.1` seconds (about 6 frames at 60 FPS). That's a common
            > starting value. Crank them up to `0.2` while you're
            > learning to feel the effect, then dial them back down.
            > Above ~`0.2`, the jump starts to feel floaty and
            > disconnected from the player's input.
        }
    }
}